result:
Fixed. The cause was in how the abstract-socket path was handed to std:

**`tokio/src/net/unix/listener.rs:82`** and **`tokio/src/net/unix/stream.rs:78`** detected the leading NUL that marks an abstract name, then passed the *whole* byte slice — NUL included — to `SocketAddr::from_abstract_name`. But that constructor prepends the NUL itself, so binding/connecting `"\0aaa"` actually registered the abstract name `"\0aaa"`, i.e. the address `"\0\0aaa"`. Reading it back gave `[0, 97, 97]` where the caller asked for `aaa`.

The fix strips the marker before passing the name along, in both `UnixListener::bind` and `UnixStream::connect`:

```rust
let os_str_bytes = path.as_ref().as_os_str().as_bytes();
if let Some(name) = os_str_bytes.strip_prefix(b"\0") {
    // `from_abstract_name` prepends the leading null byte itself,
    // so it must not be included in the name passed to it.
    StdSocketAddr::from_abstract_name(name)?
} else {
    StdSocketAddr::from_pathname(path)?
}
```

Verification:
- `uds_stream`'s `abstract_socket_name` fails on the unfixed tree with `left: [0, 97, 97], right: [97, 97, 97]`, and passes with the fix — all 8 tests in the file pass.
- `uds_socket`, `uds_datagram`, `uds_split`, and `uds_cred` all pass (17 tests total).
- `cargo clippy` isn't installed in this environment, so I couldn't run the lint pass.
